ren":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When two spacecraft want to rendezvous in space, they must simultaneously have the same position and velocity vectors. Common examples of rendezvous are resupply craft for the ISS or the Lunar Module (LM) with the Command and Service Module (CSM) during the Apollo missions.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"gfJfQWThRQ"}],"key":"iCXe4QUFDU"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For two spacecraft to rendezvous, there are typically two stages to the maneuver:","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"mhCX8Gm6Kb"}],"key":"mbz76SQVKc"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Phasing maneuver","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"QulVwUTnbQ"}],"key":"NIecSnfaA6"},{"type":"text","value":": The two spacecraft may begin the maneuver very far apart. The first stage of the rendezvous is to bring the two spacecraft into close proximity by performing a phasing maneuver, usually by only one of the spacecraft.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"nAdGcFkih5"}],"key":"QwZa93eER0"}],"key":"E1M0wye5Pb"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final approach","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"s8IxVFLgc8"}],"key":"VpJ5VogvuO"},{"type":"text","value":": Once the spacecraft are in close proximity, the final approach maneuver requires small adjustments to the approaching craft’s velocity so that the two can match.","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"KPfM9ztgqG"}],"key":"OxITdk7hTA"}],"key":"rVl2t85p3G"}],"key":"oDw1vVx59A"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this section, we are going to focus on the first stage, the phasing maneuver. A phasing maneuver, in general, is a two-impulse transfer from an orbit into a different orbit, then back to the original orbit. The period of the transfer orbit is different from the original orbit, so the spacecraft will arrive back at the original impulse point at a different time than if it had stayed on the original orbit.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"gYmaI2nhLs"}],"key":"f5BEC0NAji"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We assume that the velocity change is applied parallel to the velocity vector at the impulse point. This gives the minimum propellant usage for such a maneuver, but is relatively slow. More general orbital transfers, including rendezvous maneuvers that require less than a full orbit, will be covered in ","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"Pw4bsHph5g"},{"type":"link","url":"/orbital-maneuvers/non-hohmann-transfers","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-Hohmann Transfers with a Common Apse Line","key":"szEmHsszY7"}],"urlSource":"./non-hohmann-transfers.md","dataUrl":"/orbital-maneuvers.no